{%- comment -%}
Adapted from /home/rmdes/zachleat.com/_includes/post-list-entry.html. That
source is title-centric (year subheads with sparklines, popularity ranking,
og-image previews) built for a single post shape that always has a title.
None of that applies here: four of Indiekit's six post types (note, bookmark,
like, reply) are titleless by IndieWeb design and are the majority of what
gets published, not an edge case — a listing built around headlines would
silently drop or blank them (the same mistake that produced empty <title>
elements in the Atom feed earlier in this project, see eleventyComputed.js).

So this partial does NOT reimplement per-type rendering: it dispatches to the
same _includes/types/*.liquid partials entry.liquid already uses (Task 6),
via the identical {% case postType %} switch, so every list entry renders
its own type's feed-row shape (Task S4 branch inside each type partial, keyed
on the `lcp` flag — see below).

Task S4 (audit §2.2, corrected — see task-s4-report.md): the first pass at
this listing put an `<article class="h-entry">` inside each `<li>` and had
every type partial render its own full permalink body (the content-grid
e-content div, images, everything) into the feed row. Both were wrong:

1. The source's `.posts`/`.posts-link` selectors are descendant combinators
   off `li` (`.posts:not(.posts-withimg) li`, `.posts .posts-link`) — a
   nested <article> breaks every one of them, so h-entry moved onto the <li>
   itself, matching the source's own `<li class="h-entry">`.
2. The source's listing is a compact LINK LIST — title + date, never a post
   body — because every one of its posts has a title. Rendering the full
   e-content div into every row made the homepage a wall of repeated post
   text, not a listing. `content-grid` doubly doesn't belong here: it's a
   *permalink* layout class (Task S3 added it to the type partials' own
   content wrapper for that reason), not a listing one.

The adaptation this needs, that a straight copy of the source can't give it:
four of six Indiekit post types have no title, so a pure link list reading
"Reply · 30 Aug 2026" / "Note · 30 Aug 2026" (eleventyComputed.js's Atom-feed
fallback label, needed there because @11ty/eleventy-plugin-rss requires a
non-empty atom:title) is useless right next to a `<time>` showing the same
date. Each type partial now renders one of two shapes when `lcp` is unset
(this file never sets it — see the type partials' own comments):

  - article, bookmark: the source's link-list shape verbatim — the anchor
    IS the entry's real substance (a real title, or — for a bookmark with no
    title of its own — eleventyComputed.js's bookmark-specific fallback,
    which is the bookmarked URL itself, not the generic "<Type> · <date>"
    label, so it never duplicates the date either). No body.
  - note, like, reply, photo: always titleless, and eleventyComputed.js's
    fallback for these four IS the date-duplicating "<Type> · <date>" label.
    The permalink anchor still carries u-url/p-name (an existing guard
    requires every h-entry to have both), but its visible text is hidden
    (.sr-only — reachable to assistive tech and mf2 parsers, same technique
    the source itself uses for `.posts .date`, which is a11y-only inside
    .posts by design). What's shown instead is each type's own substance:
    note's own text (truncated, see types/note.liquid), the reply's own
    words if it has any (falling back to its target), and the like/bookmark
    target — wrapped in `.description` (sass/theme-posts.scss:933-942 in the
    source line numbers), the source's own hook for exactly this "excerpt
    under the link" presentation. Photo's substance is the photo: its
    thumbnail sits inside a plain (unmarked) link back to the permalink.

Expects a local `post` (one `collections.post` item) AND `page` overridden to
that same `post` — both passed by the caller (content/index.liquid). The
`page` override matters: isoInstant/humanDate (eleventy.config.js) re-read
the *current* page's own front matter via `this.page.inputPath` to detect an
explicit UTC offset. Looped straight from the homepage with no override,
`this.page` would stay bound to content/index.liquid (no `date:` line at
all), every post would be mis-detected as zone-less, and each already-correct
instant would be re-anchored by the configured timezone's offset — wrong, but
not obviously so (still a plausible-looking ISO string). Passing `page: post`
on the include (LiquidJS locals shadow the parent scope for that render only)
makes the filters read each post's own file, exactly as they do when that
page renders itself.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign date = post.data.date -%}
{%- assign title = post.data.title -%}
{%- assign content = post.templateContent -%}
{%- assign bookmarkOf = post.data.bookmarkOf -%}
{%- assign likeOf = post.data.likeOf -%}
{%- assign inReplyTo = post.data.inReplyTo -%}
{%- assign photo = post.data.photo -%}
{%- assign postType = post.data.postType -%}
<li class="h-entry">
	{% case postType %}
		{% when "article" %}{% include "types/article.liquid" %}
		{% when "bookmark" %}{% include "types/bookmark.liquid" %}
		{% when "like" %}{% include "types/like.liquid" %}
		{% when "photo" %}{% include "types/photo.liquid" %}
		{% when "reply" %}{% include "types/reply.liquid" %}
		{% else %}{% include "types/note.liquid" %}
	{% endcase %}
</li>
